<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Monastery of Leyre</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as San Salvador de Leyre, Leire Monastery</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Monastery of Leyre (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-salvador">San Salvador</a> de Leyre) is a monastery in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a> near Yesa, one of the oldest and most important in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. In the tenth and eleventh centuries it was closely linked to the kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamplona">Pamplona</a>, who were buried there and endowed it with great estates. Its Romanesque crypt and church date from the eleventh century, and Benedictine monks have occupied it again since 1954.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/monastery-of-leyre.jpg" alt="Monastery of Leyre" width="320" height="430" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gerd Eichmann · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Leyre-12-Kirchenwestportal-1996-gje.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
